EXPERIMENT 14: LEAP YEAR CHECKING USING JUNIT ASSERTIONS

## 1. AIM

To write a Java program to check whether a given year is a Leap Year and validate the output using JUnit assertions.

## 2. ALGORITHM

1. Start the program.
2. Read the year from the user.
3. Check whether the year is divisible by 400.
4. If yes, it is a Leap Year.
5. Otherwise, check whether the year is divisible by 100. If yes, it is not a Leap Year.
6. Otherwise, check whether the year is divisible by 4. If yes, it is a Leap Year.
7. Otherwise, it is not a Leap Year.
8. Use JUnit assertions to validate the result.
9. Display the output and stop the program.

## 3. CODE

### File 1: LeapYear.java

```java
public class LeapYear {

    public static boolean isLeapYear(int year) {

        if (year % 400 == 0) {
            return true;
        } else if (year % 100 == 0) {
            return false;
        } else if (year % 4 == 0) {
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int year = 2024;

        if (isLeapYear(year)) {
            System.out.println(year + " is a Leap Year");
        } else {
            System.out.println(year + " is not a Leap Year");
        }
    }
}
```

### File 2: LeapYearTest.java

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class LeapYearTest {

    @Test
    public void testLeapYear() {
        assertTrue(LeapYear.isLeapYear(2024));
    }

    @Test
    public void testNonLeapYear() {
        assertFalse(LeapYear.isLeapYear(2023));
    }

    @Test
    public void testCenturyLeapYear() {
        assertTrue(LeapYear.isLeapYear(2000));
    }

    @Test
    public void testCenturyNonLeapYear() {
        assertFalse(LeapYear.isLeapYear(1900));
    }
}
```

